-- `RDX0115` — Rust's method-call syntax, retired by plan 57 leaf 3.
--
-- **Both AST shapes**, which is the whole point of the fixture. §11.2
-- measured that `x.f (a)` was `Expr::MethodCall` and `x.f a` is an
-- application over an `Expr::Field`, so a refusal in one place retires one
-- spelling and leaves the other emitting the identical `x.f(a)` silently.
-- Four lines, four shapes: parenthesised with an argument, parenthesised with
-- none, parenless, and the **adjacent** spelling Rust itself writes.
--
-- The last one is what separates the two halves. Plan 79 made the `postfix`
-- refusal test for adjacency, because refusing any following `(` also refused
-- `.contains p.name (&text)` — an ordinary receiver-last call whose `(` opens
-- the *enclosing* application's next argument. So `s.len(1)` is this half and
-- the three spaced forms are `application`'s, and all four still report.

main : ()
main =
  s = String::new ()
  a = s.len (1)
  b = s.len ()
  c = s.find "h"
  d = s.len(1)
  println! \-> "#{a}"
  ()
